===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|puff}}, {{m|enm|puf}}, from {{inh|en|ang|pyf|t=a blast of wind, puff}}, {{onomatopoeic|en|title=imitative}}. Cognate with {{cog|gml|puf}}, {{m|gml|pof}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} A sharp [[exhalation]] of a small amount of [[breath]] through the mouth.
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The ability to [[breathe]] easily while [[exert]]ing oneself.
#: {{syn|en|wind}}
#: {{ux|en|out of '''puff'''}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A small quantity of [[gas]] or [[smoke]] in the air.
#: {{ux|en|'''puff''' of smoke}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A sudden [[gust]].
#* '''1674''', {{w|Thomas Flatman}}, ''Poems and Songs''
#*: to every '''puff''' of wind a slave
# {{lb|en|informal|countable}} An act of [[inhale|inhaling]] smoke from a [[cigarette]], [[cigar]] or [[pipe]].
#: {{syn|en|drag}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[flamboyant]] or alluring [[statement]] of [[praise]].
#* '''1931''', Bernard Shaw, ''Our Theatre in the Nineties'' (volume 24, page 246)
#*: {{...}} we critics were not his fellow-guests, but simply [[deadhead]]s whose business it was to "[[dress the house]]" and write '''puffs'''.
# {{lb|en|dated|slang}} A [[puffer]], one who is employed by the owner or seller of goods sold at auction to bid up the price; an act or [[scam]] of that type.
#* '''1842''', "A Paper on Puffing", ''Ainsworth's Magazine''
#*: Is nothing to be said in praise of the "Emporiums" and "Repositories" and "Divans," which formerly were mere insignificant tailors', toymen's, and tobacconists' shops? Is the transition from the barber's pole to the revolving bust of the perruquier, nothing? — the leap from the bare counter-traversed shop to the carpeted and mirrored saloon of trade, nothing? Are they not, one and all, practical '''puffs''', intended to invest commerce with elegance, and to throw a halo round extravagance?
#* '''1848''', Mrs. White, "Puffs and Puffing", in ''Sharpe's London Magazine''
#*: Here the duke is made the vehicle of the tailor's advertisement, and the prelusive compliments, ostensibly meant for his grace, merge into a covert recommendation of the coat. Several specimens might be given of this species of '''puff''', which is to be met with in almost every paper, and is a favourite form with booksellers, professional men, &c.
#* '''2008''', David Paton-Williamspage, ''Katterfelto'', page xii
#*: He was the eighteenth century king of spin, or, in the language of the day, the "prince of '''puff'''".
# A [[puffball]].
#* '''1598''', {{w|John Florio}}, ''A Worlde of Wordes, or Most Copious, and Exact Dictionarie in Italian and English'', London: Edward Blount, p. 47,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A00991.0001.001]</sup>
#*: ''Bozzacchio'', an acorne. Also a '''puffe''' or [[mushrump]] full of dust.
# A [[powder puff]].
# {{lb|en|uncountable|slang}} The [[drug]] [[cannabis]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:marijuana}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A light [[cake]] filled with cream, cream cheese, etc.
#: {{syn|en|pastry|cream puff}}
#: {{ux|en|cream '''puff'''}}
# {{lb|en|derogatory|slang|British|particularly northern UK}} {{syn of|en|poof|nodot=a}}: a [[male]] [[homosexual]], [[especially]] an [[effeminate]] [[one]].
# {{lb|en|slang|dated|UK}} [[life|Life]].
#* '''1938''', {{w|P. G. Wodehouse}}, ''The Code of the Woosters''
#*: Did you ever in your '''puff''' see such a perfect perisher?
# A portion of [[fabric]] gathered up so as to be left full in the middle.
#: ''a sleeve with a '''puff''' at the shoulder''
# {{lb|en|genetics}} A region of a [[chromosome]] exhibiting a local [[increase]] in [[diameter]].

