===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|puffen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|pyffan|t=to breathe out, blow with the mouth}}. Compare {{cog|nl|puffen}}, {{cog|nds-de|puffen}}, {{cog|de|puffen}}, {{cog|da|puffe}}, {{cog|sv|puffa}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To emit smoke, gas, etc., in puffs.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[pant]].
#* '''1692''', {{w|Roger L'Estrange}}, '' Fables of Aesop and Other Eminent Mythologists''
#*: By and by comes the ass back again, '''Puffing''' and Blowing, from the Chase.
#* '''1918''', {{w|Edgar Rice Burroughs}}, [[w:The Land That Time Forgot (novel)|The Land That Time Forgot]] Chapter VI
#*: '''Puffing''' and panting, we plodded on until within about a mile of the harbor we came upon a sight that brought us all up standing.
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[advertise]].
# To blow as an expression of scorn.
#* {{RQ:South 12}}
#*: It is really to defy Heaven to '''puff''' at damnation.
# To swell with air; to be dilated or inflated.
#: {{rfquotek|en|Boyle}}
# To breathe in a swelling, inflated, or pompous manner; hence, to assume importance.
#* '''1633''', {{w|George Herbert}}, ''The Quip''
#*: Then came brave Glory '''puffing''' by.
# To drive with a puff, or with puffs.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Virgil|Georgic 1}}
#*: The clearing north will '''puff''' the clouds away.
# To repel with words; to blow at contemptuously.
#* '''1685''', {{w|John Dryden}}, ''The Twenty-Ninth Ode of the First Book of Horace''
#*: I '''puff''' the prostitute away.
# To cause to swell or dilate; to inflate.
#: ''a bladder '''puffed''' with air''
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Shrew|I|ii|passage=the sea '''puffed''' up with winds}}
# To inflate with pride, flattery, self-esteem, etc.; often with ''up''.
#* '''1881''', {{w|Benjamin Jowett}}, ''Thucydides Translated into English''
#*: '''puffed''' up with military success
# To praise with exaggeration; to flatter; to call public attention to by praises; to praise unduly.
#* {{RQ:Macaulay Johnson|passage='''puffed''' with wonderful skill}}

